  "problem": "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find $\\,T(9{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(9{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |",
  "worked_solution": "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2} & =13{v}_{1}-4{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2} \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}-4({v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}-4(0) \\\\  & =13{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(9{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2})=T(13{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(13{v}_{1}) & =13T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =13[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-26 \\\\ 65 \\\\ -78\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(9{v}_{1}-4{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-26 \\\\ 65 \\\\ -78\\end{aligned}].$",
